course: dictionary
lesson: 712
title: Bargain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bargain can mean a good deal, a promise, or the talking that leads to a price. Look at the words around it to find out which. «Toad bargains for six shillings and sixpence and as much stew as he can eat, and gets it.» Here bargains is a verb: Toad talks until both sides agree on a price he likes.
explain: A bargain is something you buy for less than its usual price. «A sale lowers prices; a bargain is only a bargain if you would have bought the thing anyway.» Cheap alone is not enough. A real bargain is something you actually want.
explain: In a story, a bargain is a promise that each side agrees to keep. «The Woman makes a bargain: one word in his praise and he may come into the Cave...» The Woman sets the terms, and both sides must keep their part.
explain: Be careful with rushed adverts. «Hurry, only today!» and countdowns push you to decide fast, before you think clearly. Real bargains rarely vanish in minutes, so stop, check, and ask whether you really need the thing.
fact: A bargain is a good deal, something bought for less than its usual price.
fact: A bargain can also be an agreement, where each person promises to do something.
fact: To bargain means to talk with someone to agree a price or deal, trying to get a better one.
fact: Phrases: strike a bargain means agree a deal; keep your side of the bargain means do what you promised; more than you bargained for means more trouble than you expected.
exercise: «Toad bargains for six shillings and sixpence and as much stew as he can eat.» What does bargains mean here? | Sings a song about money | Runs away from the price | Buys the stew at the shop's usual price | *Talks until both sides agree a price
exercise: «Hurry, only today!» is a sales trick. What is the best thing to do? | Buy it quickly before it goes | Buy two so you save more | *Stop, check, and ask if you really need it | Tell everyone about the sale
exercise: «The Woman makes a bargain: one word in his praise and he may come into the Cave.» What does a bargain mean here? | A cave where you can sleep | A sale with a big discount | To argue loudly about the price | *An agreement in which each side promises something
test: «She bargained with the stallholder for a lower price.» What does bargained mean? | *To talk with someone to agree a better price | To hide the price from everyone | To pay the full price with no talk | To buy something that is broken
test: Which phrase means "do what you promised"? | Bargain hunting | Strike a bargain | More than you bargained for | *Keep your side of the bargain
exam: Mia says a bargain is always cheap. Using «A sale lowers prices; a bargain is only a bargain if you would have bought the thing anyway», who is right? | *Not quite: cheap only counts if you would have bought it anyway | No, bargains are always expensive | Yes, anything cheap is a bargain | Yes, but only at a sale on a Tuesday
